NativeAOT: with a whole-program scan, drop the fat function pointer check from static calli sites that no fat
pointer can reach. Applied after perf-43; ILC only, not RISC-V specific.
- A calli compiled for NativeAOT tests its target for the fat pointer bit and, when set, loads the real target
  and passes the instantiation argument first. The JIT homes the arguments for that layout, so the common thin
  path pays for it too: in Nethermind's interpreter, where every opcode handler ends by dispatching the next one,
  POP's handler runs 35 instructions with the check (9 of them register moves) and 23 without.
- ScannedDevirtualizationManager records the parameter count of every static method a fat pointer can target:
  FatFunctionPointerNode (ldftn and delegate targets in unshared code, dictionary entries computed at compile
  time, class constructor contexts), MethodEntry and ConstrainedDirectCall generic lookups in shared code (the
  entries the type loader fills when it builds a dictionary at run time) and every reflectable method
  (RuntimeMethodHandle.GetFunctionPointer; ldtoken of a method makes it reflectable).
- IsFatPointerCandidate returns false for a calli in IL (an EcmaMethod) with a static signature whose parameter
  count none of those targets has. Instance signatures and parameterless ones keep the check: instance fat
  pointers also come from GVM resolution and value type methods, and ClassConstructorRunner calls class
  constructors, fat for shared generic types, through a static parameterless calli. Compiler-generated stubs
  and compilations without a scan keep it too.

